 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Yes. Eating out in Melbourne costs more than it did a year ago, and prices are still rising at a fairly steady pace.\n\n**Melbourne, meals out and takeaway (ABS CPI)**\n- **Latest, August 2026:** prices are 3.5% higher than in August 2025.\n- **Index level:** it rose from 99.82 in August 2025 to 103.29 in August 2026.\n- **Recent range:** annual growth has been 3.3% to 4.4% since late 2025. It peaked at 4.4% in May 2026 and was 4.1% in July 2026.\n- **Longer view:** the annual rate was 5.8% in March quarter 2024. It slowed to about 2.3–2.6% through 2025, then picked up again.\n\n**Compared with the rest of the country**\n- Australia-wide, meals out rose 4.1% over the year to August 2026 (4.5% in July). Melbourne's 3.5% is a bit lower.\n- Melbourne's 3.5% is about the same as overall inflation in the city. Its all-groups index rose from 99.54 to 103.04 over the same period, which is also about 3.5%.\n\nEating out is still getting dearer each year. The pace is slower than in early 2024 but faster than the low point in mid-2025.",
